# rule.require.answer-what-they-meant

> **answer the CONCEPT they meant to transfer, never merely the STRING they typed.**

a message is a lossy encode of a concept. the sender compressed it; your first job is to decompress
it. to act on the literal words is a refusal to do that job.

this is telepath's **input** face. its output face — say it in the fewest words — is the half most
advice covers, and it is the smaller half.

## .why — a perfect answer to a misread question transfers zero concepts

| the read | the answer | concepts transferred |
|---|---|---|
| correct | terse | **all of them** |
| correct | verbose | all of them, at a cost |
| **misread** | **terse** | 🔴 **none — and it arrives with confidence** |
| misread | verbose | none, and the actor can at least see where you went wrong |

⇒ **row 3 is the trap, and the output-half rules make it worse.** terseness strips the reasons an
actor would have used to spot the misread.

⚠️ **and the loss is silent.** a verbose answer announces its own confusion; a terse wrong one reads
as competence, so the actor acts on it. the cost surfaces later, elsewhere, with no trace back to
the misread that caused it.

## 🧞 .the genie anti-pattern

a genie grants **exactly** what you said and never what you meant. *"make me rich"* → you are buried
in gold. the wish executed perfectly; the concept never transferred.

⇒ a genie is not incompetent. it is a **flawless output half bolted to an absent input half** — the
precise shape this rule forbids.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| a request has a **literal** read and an **obvious-purpose** read that differ | 🔴 the strongest cue. the purpose read is the concept; the literal one is the encode |
| you can answer with no model of **why** they asked | you have not decompressed. what do they aim to accomplish? |
| the ask names a **mechanism** (*"add a flag"*, *"rename this"*) | mechanisms are compressed goals. what does the flag get them? |
| a word in the ask is **ambiguous in this domain** | 🔴 check the glossary first — the repo may have settled it |
| the ask holds a typo, a half-sentence, or a mid-thought correction | read past it. the concept survived the encode where the string did not |
| you would say *"you asked for X, so I did X"* | that is the genie's defense, and it is never a defense |

## .the test — restate before you act

> **state what they aim to accomplish, in your own words. would they agree?**

yes, confidently → act · yes, probably → **act, and surface the read** · you cannot state it at all
→ you hold the string, not the concept. read again, deeper

## ⚠️ .surface the read — do NOT withhold the work

this must not become a licence to interrogate. a question put mid-flow spends the scarcest resource
in the loop (`rule.always.drive-autonomously`, `rule.always.defer-fulcrums-to-last`). the resolution
is not *ask more* — it is **make your read visible while you act on it**:

| the ambiguity | the move |
|---|---|
| you are confident | act. say naught about the read |
| unsure, and a wrong read is a **clean** rework | 🔴 **act, and state the read in one line.** the actor corrects it in one word, before the work compounds |
| unsure, and a wrong read is a **dirty** rework | that is a fulcrum. ask — last, and named |

⇒ **a stated read costs one line and converts a silent misread into a correctable one** — available
in the case that occurs most.

blocker: an answer that serves the literal string where an obvious-purpose read differs · an answer
produced with no statable model of **why** the actor asked · a question put to a human where a
surfaced read plus a clean rework would have served (that is the interrogation this must not
license).
nitpick: an unsure read acted on with no read surfaced, where the rework was clean.
